<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Lucia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Lucia is an island country in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-islands">Windward Islands</a> of the Lesser Antilles. Arawaks settled it around 200 to 400 CE and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> took it over around 800 CE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> claimed it in 1650 and signed a treaty with the Caribs in 1660, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and France then fought repeatedly over it, so that control changed often until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> secured it in 1814. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">Universal suffrage</a> came in 1951, the island belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> Federation from 1958 to 1962, and it became internally self-governing in 1967. Saint Lucia gained independence peacefully on 22 February 1979, remaining a Commonwealth realm.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The island covers 617 square kilometres and is volcanic and very mountainous. Its highest point is Mount Gimie, at 950 metres, and the two volcanic plugs called the Pitons form its best-known landmark. The Qualibou depression in the south-west holds the geothermal field of Sulphur Springs. The climate is tropical, with a dry season from 1 December to 31 May. Saint Lucia is named after Saint Lucy of Syracuse; a legend says French sailors were shipwrecked there on 13 December, her feast day, but a Vatican globe of 1520 shows the island as Sancta Lucia, which suggests that early Spanish explorers named it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first proven inhabitants were the Arawaks, who came from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> around 200 to 400 CE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> arrived around 800 CE and seized control by killing the Arawak men and assimilating the women. A Spanish cédula of 1511 mentions the island within the Spanish domain.</p><p>The French were the first Europeans to settle, and they signed a treaty with the Caribs in 1660. The English took control in 1663. Once the slave-based <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> industry developed, both powers wanted the island, and over the 18th century it changed ownership or was declared neutral a dozen times, though French settlements remained. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> fought for it 14 times in all, which earned it the name Helen of the West. Britain took complete control in 1814.</p><p>Representative government began in 1924, and universal suffrage followed in 1951. On 9 March 1942 a German U-boat sank two British ships in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castries">Castries</a> harbour. The island belonged to the West Indies Federation from 1958 to 1962 and became independent on 22 February 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Saint Lucia is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> and Commonwealth realm with a House of Assembly and a Senate. Its capital and largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castries">Castries</a>, and the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 172,948 people. English is the official language, but Saint Lucian French Creole, Kwéyòl, is spoken by a majority. About half of the population is Catholic. The island is divided into ten districts. Two Nobel laureates came from it: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-arthur-lewis">Sir Arthur Lewis</a>, in Economics in 1979, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derek-walcott">Derek Walcott</a>, in Literature in 1992.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saint Lucia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
